# ui.artbloom — environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env*` is gitignored with `!.env.example`, so
# this file is the one that gets committed and it holds NAMES ONLY: never paste a
# key, a token or a URL with credentials in it here.
#
# NOTHING BELOW IS REQUIRED TO BUILD OR DEPLOY. With every value empty the site
# builds, renders and installs exactly as it does today: install and save counts
# fall back to the `installs` / `bookmarks` fields in `lib/registry/items.ts` (all
# 17 currently zero), sign-in reports itself as not configured, and every write
# becomes a no-op. Fill these in when you want the numbers to be real.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ IS A PROMISE, NOT A CONVENTION. Next inlines any variable with that
# prefix into the JavaScript sent to the browser, where it is readable by anyone who
# opens devtools. Everything without the prefix stays on the server. Moving a value
# from the second group to the first is not a formatting change; for the secret key
# below it is a total compromise of the database.


# ─── site ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PUBLIC. Absolute origin used for registry URLs, canonical links and OpenGraph
# tags — e.g. https://ui.artbloom.com. Read at build time by `lib/brand.ts`, which
# falls back to Vercel's own production URL and then to `origin` in `brand.json`,
# so a preview deploy is fine without it. No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_ORIGIN=

# Set by Vercel, not by you: VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL is read as the fallback
# above. Listed here only so nobody wonders where it comes from.


# ─── supabase: public ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API. Both of these end up in the browser bundle
# and that is correct — they identify the project, they do not authorise anything.
# What they can reach is decided by row level security in `supabase/schema.sql`.

# PUBLIC. Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijklmnop.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# PUBLIC. The anon key (legacy JWT) or the newer publishable key. Set whichever one
# your dashboard offers — the app reads ANON first, then PUBLISHABLE, and treats the
# first non-empty one as the answer. Setting both is harmless; setting neither means
# the site runs in its unconfigured fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=


# ─── supabase: server only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SECRET. NEVER PREFIX THIS WITH NEXT_PUBLIC_. NEVER SEND IT TO A BROWSER.
#
# The secret key (or the legacy service_role key — either name is read, SECRET
# first). It bypasses row level security completely: with this key every policy in
# `supabase/schema.sql` is invisible and the caller is effectively `postgres` at a
# psql prompt. It exists for exactly one operation in this codebase — the
# `increment_install()` call in `lib/analytics/installs.ts`, which is the only
# grantee of that function — and it is loaded in `lib/analytics/server.ts`, which
# starts with `import "server-only"` so that a client component importing it at any
# depth fails the build instead of shipping the key.
#
# If it ever leaks: rotate it in the dashboard. Anything else is theatre.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# SECRET, and it is a salt rather than a credential — a long random string, 32+
# characters, from anywhere you like (`openssl rand -hex 32`).
#
# Install reports are deduplicated per address per day, and the address is stored as
# a salted SHA-256 (`lib/analytics/ip.ts`), never in the clear. The salt is what
# stops a dump of `install_events` from being testable against a guessed IP: without
# it, 4.3 billion candidate hashes is an afternoon.
#
# Unset is safe but weak: the app generates a random salt per process, warns once,
# and dedup then resets on every deploy and does not carry between serverless
# instances. Either name is read, TRACK first.
INSTALL_TRACK_SALT=
INSTALL_IP_SALT=


# ─── after you fill these in ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#  1. Run `supabase/schema.sql` in the SQL editor. It is re-runnable, so running it
#     twice is a no-op rather than a mistake.
#  2. Add the GitHub OAuth provider (Authentication → Providers) and put this
#     deployment's `/auth/callback` in the redirect allow-list, or sign-in will
#     round-trip to GitHub and come back to nowhere.
#  3. Redeploy. NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are baked into the bundle at build time, so a
#     key pasted into the dashboard does nothing until the next build.
